	"peak": 1750,
	"summary": {
		"text": "Founded, by tradition, by Oranmiyan, a prince of Ife, Oyo grew on the northern savanna into the largest of the Yoruba kingdoms. Nupe drove its kings into exile around 1535; they came back on horseback, rebuilt Oyo-Ile under Abipa, and over the next two centuries made Oyo an empire whose cavalry took tribute from Dahomey and the Aja and Ewe states to the coast. From 1754 the Basorun Gaha made and unmade kings, and Abiodun's later defeats by Borgu and Nupe, Afonja's revolt at Ilorin and the jihad broke the empire. Oyo-Ile was abandoned in the 1830s, and Alaafin Atiba rebuilt the kingdom at New Oyo, leaning on Ibadan and Ijaye to guard it.",
